For those at ICLR and interested in encoders and all things bidirectional, the poster presentation is right now. 

@N1colAIs: "We are currently presenting 'Should We Still Pretrain Encoders with Masked Language Modeling?' Come see us in Hall 3 #1304 @iclr_conf." 

The linked paper discusses the relevance of masked language modeling in pretraining encoders.

Always the same concept: optimize for the priors you actually have. Scale and model capacity enable removing inductive biases like parsers. Using vision for webpage indexing is framework agnostic: it doesn't need to be HTML.

@YichuanM highlights that the web was never meant to be flattened into text, yet most web retrieval-augmented generation systems start by parsing HTML, which is complex and lossy. PixelRAG introduces a system that retrieves and reads over 30 million web pages as pixels, using a visual language model (VLM) to read screenshots directly instead of extracting text.

PixelRAG preserves visual information and outperforms text-based retrieval on text-only question-answering benchmarks by 18.1%. This is because HTML-to-text conversion often discards layout, structure, and other signals, while a VLM trained on web page screenshots can understand web pages more accurately and efficiently. Recent advances in VLMs make them surprisingly effective at web page comprehension, suggesting that HTML parsing may be a major bottleneck in web retrieval systems.

I will be in Seoul with @marcantallard this week for ICML to present ModernVBERT, a vision-language encoder that matches the performance of models ten times its size on visual document retrieval tasks.

Our poster session is on Wednesday from 10:30PM in Hall A. Come chat with us!

